    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Yamaha Sirius Rental in Hanoi</h1>
    <p>The Yamaha Sirius shares the cheapest tier of our fleet with the Honda Wave, and it is the model locals reach for when they want a durable workhorse with a slightly different character. This guide covers the verified Yamaha Sirius rental price in Hanoi, what it is like to ride a semi-automatic in the city, and how to decide between the Sirius and the other bikes we rent.</p>
    <p>All rates below come from the published <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, the complete source of verified prices. The Sirius is part of the model lineup described in the main <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> guide.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">What the Yamaha Sirius is</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">Yamaha Sirius rental prices in Hanoi</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">Period pricing: day, week and month</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">Sirius versus Honda Wave</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">Semi-automatic riding for newcomers</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-6">Practical life with a Sirius</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-7">How to book a Yamaha Sirius</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">What the Yamaha Sirius is</h2>
    <p>The Sirius is Yamaha's underbone bike for the same job the Honda Wave does: cheap, simple, everyday transport that survives years of hard use. It has a foot-operated gear lever and no hand clutch, a flat seat, a rack and a fuel-efficient small engine. You see them all over Hanoi carrying deliveries, market goods and commuters.</p>
    <p>For a renter, that DNA translates into three things. The bike is light enough to push and park easily. The parts and service network around it is enormous, because half the city rides similar machines. And the riding position is upright and natural, with a seat height most riders manage without trouble. None of this is glamorous, and that is exactly the point of the category.</p>
    <p>If you are new to Vietnam's bike categories, the choice is simpler than the model names suggest: semi-automatic underbones like the Sirius and Wave, automatic scooters like the <a href="/hanoi-scooter-rental/">scooters in our fleet</a>, and the light 50cc machines covered in the <a href="/hanoi-50cc-motorbike-rental/">50cc motorbike rental</a> guide.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">Yamaha Sirius rental prices in Hanoi</h2>
    <p>The verified Yamaha Sirius rental price in Hanoi is 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week, and between 900,000 and 1,200,000 VND per month. The monthly rate is a confirmed range; contact us for the exact current monthly price for your dates and the specific bike available.</p>
    <p>These are the same figures published for the Honda Wave, which makes the Sirius one of the two cheapest bikes in the fleet. Every number can be cross-checked on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">full price list</a> before you book.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Period pricing: day, week and month</h2>
    <p>The period you choose changes the total more than the model does. A week of daily Sirius rentals would cost 1,050,000 VND against the 700,000 VND weekly rate. A month of daily rentals would cost 4,500,000 VND against the 900,000&ndash;1,200,000 VND monthly range, roughly a quarter of the stacked price.</p>
    <p>Per day, the weekly rate works out to 100,000 VND and the monthly rate to between 30,000 and 40,000 VND. For stays longer than a week, the monthly option is the obvious financial move; the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental in Hanoi</a> hub explains how those longer rentals work in practice.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Sirius versus Honda Wave</h2>
    <p>The Sirius and the Wave rent for identical verified rates, so the comparison is character, not cost. Riders who try both usually describe the difference as feel: the Wave is the default choice with the largest installed base, while the Sirius is the alternative for riders who prefer Yamaha's styling or simply like the way it sits.</p>
    <p>Functionally the bikes are interchangeable. Both are semi-automatic underbones, both carry the same tier of weekly and monthly pricing, and both are appropriate for beginners who accept shifting gears with their left foot. When both are available, sit on each and pick the one that feels right; when one is taken, the other loses you nothing. That is rare in a price list, and worth using: at 150,000 VND per day, the semi-automatic tier undercuts the automatic Honda Click and Yamaha Mio at the same price while offering more traditional bikes.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Semi-automatic riding for newcomers</h2>
    <p>Riding the Sirius means managing four gears with your left foot and nothing with your left hand. The pattern is simple: ease off the throttle, press the lever down for higher gears, up for lower, and reapply the throttle. There is no clutch to slip and no way to stall in the usual sense, which is why semi-automatics are considered beginner-friendly in Vietnam despite the gears.</p>
    <p>The learning curve is an afternoon, not a week. Start on quiet streets, practice shifting up and down through the full range, and add traffic once the pattern is automatic in your feet. Two habits from local riders are worth copying: shift early rather than revving high, and use both brakes together smoothly. For a fuller primer on city riding, browse the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> hub, and for the pickup and inspection process read <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/how-to-rent-a-motorbike-in-hanoi/">how to rent a motorbike in Hanoi</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">Practical life with a Sirius</h2>
    <p>The Sirius is happiest doing everyday jobs: commuting, errands, and paved day trips out of the city. Fuel costs stay modest on a small underbone, and fuel stations are dense across Hanoi, so planning around fuel is rarely necessary inside the city. Storage is a rack rather than a trunk, so plan for a backpack or a bungee cord rather than under-seat space.</p>
    <p>Where the Sirius earns its keep is distance-per-dong on longer stays. A month on the cheapest tier keeps transport costs minimal, and the bike's simplicity means less to go wrong. Riders planning routes out of the city can start from the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/">Hanoi motorbike trips</a> hub for ideas and planning advice.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">How to book a Yamaha Sirius</h2>
    <p>Call <a href="tel:+84942467674">0942 467 674</a>, email <a href="mailto:nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com">nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com</a>, or visit the shop at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi. We are open 09:00&ndash;21:00 daily, and the rental is completed at the shop. Contact us ahead of your dates so we can confirm a Sirius is available.</p>
    <p>Decide your rental period before you arrive, and for anything about deposits, documents or conditions, ask us directly or check the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> hub first.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-8">The Sirius in Hanoi life: routes and reality</h2>
    <p>It helps to picture what a week with the Sirius actually looks like. Day one is pickup and the first careful loops around Long Bien. Day two, the ride into the center becomes routine: across the bridge or down the dyke road, into the Old Quarter's orbit, and a first try at the pavement parking strips. By day three, the gears are muscle memory and the bike has become the default answer to every question the city asks, from where to eat to how to reach a museum before it closes.</p>
    <p>That is the pattern for every bike category, but the Sirius gets there with the least ceremony. There is nothing to configure, nothing to be careful with, and nothing to learn beyond the foot lever. Renters who were nervous about riding in Vietnam consistently report the same thing by mid-week: the traffic reads as chaotic from a sidewalk and as a rhythm from a seat, and a simple bike lets you concentrate entirely on that rhythm.</p>
    <p>The Sirius also shrinks the map. Distances that feel long on foot, such as the stretch between the Old Quarter and the Long Bien side of the river, are minutes on an underbone, and the ring around West Lake becomes a normal evening option rather than an excursion. Riders who keep the bike for a month end up reorganizing their whole stay around the mobility, which is the strongest argument for the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly rates</a> over stacking days.</p>
    <p>If this guide has described a bike that sounds right, the next step is simple: check availability for your dates, reconfirm the rates on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">price hub</a>, and read <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/how-to-rent-a-motorbike-in-hanoi/">how the rental process works</a> so the pickup itself is quick.</p>

    <p>One last word on expectations, because the Sirius asks to be judged fairly. It is not fast, not plush and not stylish, and riders who want any of those things should rent the premium automatics instead, at 200,000 VND per day. What the Sirius offers is the cheapest verified mobility in the fleet with the least that can go wrong, which is a trade a great many riders, from students to long-stay expats to first-time visitors, conclude is exactly right. Confirm your dates and period with us, verify the figures on the price hub, and the Sirius will do the rest quietly and without fuss.</p>

    <p>To book, call <a href="tel:+84942467674">0942 467 674</a> or email <a href="mailto:nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com">nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com</a>, or simply come to the shop at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, any day between 09:00 and 21:00. Tell us the dates and the period you have in mind, ask any question this guide has not answered, and the rental is completed at the shop while you wait. That is the whole process, deliberately simple, exactly like the bike.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about Yamaha Sirius rental</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>How much is a Yamaha Sirius rental in Hanoi?</h2>
      <p>The verified rates are 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week and 900,000&ndash;1,200,000 VND per month. The monthly figure is a confirmed range, so contact the shop for the exact price for your dates.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Which is better, the Yamaha Sirius or the Honda Wave?</h2>
      <p>They rent for the same verified rates and do the same job. The Wave has the larger installed base; the Sirius is the Yamaha alternative. If both are available, choose whichever feels better when you sit on it.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Is the Sirius hard to learn for a new rider?</h2>
      <p>No. Semi-automatic bikes have a foot lever and no hand clutch, so most riders are comfortable within a day. Start on quiet streets and add city traffic once the gear pattern feels natural.</p>
    </article>

